**Verdict:** `revise` — trust strained, value high, delight clear_hit. **4 blockers, 5 concerns.**

The draft has genuine protective instincts (it refuses to convert his self-described OCD into type evidence, withholds motive on the Alice band, holds the Brooklyn dispute open, gives him his 2002 *Attitude* record *before* the Qatar charge). The failure is narrower: in four places it reaches past what Beckham has said on the record — in sources it already cites — and substitutes a more calculating man. Every unsourced addition tilts the same direction, which is the pattern worth naming.

**Strongest hit:** *"Whether he planned it or not, a private humiliation had become a photograph on his own terms."* The one place the draft had every incentive to assert calculation and explicitly declined — the thesis rendered as behavior with an unknown motive, which is the only honest form it can take.

**Highest-priority concern (SUBJECT-R1, blocker):** The draft asks how Beckham survived 1998, presents him as unable to answer ("I don't know"), and answers for him — "He delivered." He answered it himself, unprompted, four times in the very episode the draft quotes four times: *"it didn't feel tough at the time because I had the right people around me I had a manager that looked after me a team that looked after me and... teammates that... had my back."* I grepped the snapshot to confirm no body passage mentions that support before asserting the omission. His account is relational; the article's is autarkic — and the draft commits to the second in `meta_title`, `persona_title`, and description ("The Boy Who Built Himself," "Football's Self-Made Idol"). The related truncation cuts his therapist quote one clause before he credits those people and endorses therapy for others.

Two notes on the research beyond the packet:

- I **verified** Victoria's April 2026 WSJ interview (published 16 April), which the packet flagged as unconfirmed — the draft's paraphrase is accurate. No finding raised.
- I found a first-person correction the packet missed: Beckham has directly rejected the marriage-as-brand reading on BBC Radio 4 (*"Of course not. We stay together because we love each other"*). The draft asserts that reading in its own voice, and it's the same proposition it calls unknowable three paragraphs later when Brooklyn alleges it.

I also independently confirmed the packet's two provenance disputes from the primary transcript — the live studio reaction lands *after* both the Keane "inhumane" line and Beckham's "I don't know," so both sit inside clip playback and two narrated scenes did not occur as written.
